WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.264
Two threads settle payments for one merchant at once.

2
00:00:06.264 --> 00:00:10.299
The totals live in a ConcurrentHashMap, the thread-safe map.

3
00:00:10.299 --> 00:00:12.384
A adds 20 cents, and B adds 30.

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.129
Each one runs this line: get the total, add its cents, put the sum back.

5
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:19.704
A calls get, and reads 500.

6
00:00:19.704 --> 00:00:23.832
Before A puts, B calls get, and also reads 500.

7
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:25.033
A puts 520.

8
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:26.849
Then B puts its sum.

9
00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:28.939
What does the map hold?

10
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:31.877
530.

11
00:00:31.877 --> 00:00:33.768
A's 20 cents are gone.

12
00:00:33.768 --> 00:00:36.003
Each call was thread-safe.

13
00:00:36.003 --> 00:00:41.160
The pair was not: it's the read, add, write race of count++.

14
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:44.880
To see why, look inside the map.

15
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.539
It's an array of bins.

16
00:00:46.539 --> 00:00:50.385
A key's hash picks its bin, so m-17 lands in bin 3.

17
00:00:50.385 --> 00:00:52.648
One bin can hold several keys.

18
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:54.028
Reads never lock.

19
00:00:54.028 --> 00:00:58.330
A write locks only its own bin, for one call, and then lets go.

20
00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:02.700
Into an empty bin, it uses compare-and-set, with no lock at all.

21
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:08.090
So get takes no lock, and put holds the bin only while it writes.

22
00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:13.751
Between A's get and A's put, nothing holds bin 3, and B reads the same old 500.

23
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:17.343
Now use merge.

24
00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:21.163
It reads, adds and writes inside one lock of the bin.

25
00:01:21.163 --> 00:01:26.209
Its Javadoc says the entire method invocation is performed atomically.

26
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:29.264
A holds bin 3 in the middle of its merge.

27
00:01:29.264 --> 00:01:31.680
B's merge arrives for the same key.

28
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.853
What will B read?

29
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:37.322
B waits at the bin.

30
00:01:37.322 --> 00:01:39.807
A writes 520 and lets go.

31
00:01:39.807 --> 00:01:44.579
Then B takes the bin, reads 520, and writes 550.

32
00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:46.169
Nothing is lost.

33
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.620
Meanwhile, C updates m-42 in bin 6, and never meets A.

34
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.668
Only writes to the same bin take turns.

35
00:01:53.668 --> 00:01:56.169
D reads m-5 with no lock at all.

36
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.325
In code, the two calls become one.

37
00:01:59.325 --> 00:02:04.797
merge puts the cents in, or adds them to the old total, atomically for that key.

38
00:02:04.797 --> 00:02:07.943
compute and computeIfAbsent work the same way.

39
00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:12.955
So one call is atomic for its key, and two calls are two steps.

40
00:02:12.955 --> 00:02:16.545
Do each read, modify, write on a key in a single call.

41
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:17.569
Quick check.

42
00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:20.713
Two threads run: if the key is missing, put it.

43
00:02:20.713 --> 00:02:22.920
Both run it for the same new key.

44
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.324
Can both of them put?
